An archetype, not a replica
---------------------------

This cartridge models a FAMILY — two rigid decks on a knuckle hinge along the
long edge, a display in each, controls and ports on the lower deck — which is
ours to license. It does not model any manufacturer's product.

Named devices appear only as PRESETS. A preset is a set of numbers, and every
number in it is either:

  * a DIMENSION THE MANUFACTURER PUBLISHED, carried from Telesia's device
    geometry registry (`data/registry/device-geometry.json`), where each value
    records its source tier, URL, retrieval date and the line it was quoted
    from; or

  * a value DERIVED from such a dimension by an archetype rule that is OURS and
    is written down (docs/README.md, "Archetype derivation rules"); or

  * an ARCHETYPE DEFAULT that is ours outright.

Nothing else. In particular this cartridge reproduces no logo, no wordmark, no
badge, no colourway, no textures, no vents, no parting lines, no button glyphs,
and no surface detail of any kind beyond what a published outline, a published
display active area, a published control count and a published port list imply.
It is deliberately a plain rounded slab: the shape you get is the archetype's,
not the product's.

**A preset is a fit envelope, not a likeness.** It is there so a cradle, a dock,
a travel shell or a repair fixture can be built to the right size.


What is ours, specifically
--------------------------

These are the archetype's own decisions. They are not, and must not be read as,
statements about any device:

  * The split of the published closed thickness into an upper and a lower deck.
    The SUM is the manufacturer's figure; the 45/55 split is ours.
  * Corner radius. No manufacturer in this family publishes one — 0 of the
    registry's 84 devices — so every corner radius here is ours.
  * Hinge barrel radius, running clearance, knuckle count, knuckle length and
    the extent of the hinge run.
  * Display recess depth, and where each display sits within its deck (the
    presets centre it; no manufacturer publishes a bezel split).
  * Every control's size and position — the registry records how many buttons a
    device has, never how large they are or where they sit.
  * The port opening sizes, and where along a face they sit. The 3.5 mm figure
    is the connector's nominal diameter, which is the registry's own port kind.
  * The cartridge slot in its entirety: the registry's port vocabulary has no
    game-card kind, so nothing about this component is sourced for any preset.
  * The speaker grille in its entirety.


Devices with no preset
----------------------

Three devices the registry files in this family have no preset, because their
closed envelope is unresolved and this cartridge will not invent one:

  * AYN Thor (`ayn-thor`) — AYN publishes no dimensions and no mass on its own
    site. Telesia's plan gates it on a calibrated measurement of a physical unit
    (`docs/DEVICE_GEOMETRY.md` gate H7).
  * Sony Tablet P (`sony-tablet-p`) — every Sony page refused to be read; the
    entry has zero sources.
  * OneXPlayer ONEXSUGAR Sugar 1 (`onexplayer-onexsugar`) — no closed dimensions
    from the manufacturer.

`null = unresolved, never guessed`.


